 "market_notes": {
  "2025": "Few teams had cap space, so the market was quiet; Turner (4y/107M) was the top outside signing, most stars re-signed or extended, and many mid-tier players (Kuminga, Beal, Paul, Ayton) took short or cheap deals. Characterisation inferred from deal sizes, not quoted from a source.",
  "2026": "Cap rose to 164.961M; most big money went to re-signings and extensions (Young 212M, Duren 200M, Reaves 185M, Mitchell 273M extension) while several veterans took deep discounts (LeBron 2y/8M with PHI, Beal, Klay, Kuminga 2y/12.4M). Characterisation inferred from tracker data."
 },
 "notes": "Ages as printed by trackers (CBS 2025 ages may be ~1 year high; many extension ages null). AAV = total/years (calculated). kind=extension marks extensions/rookie extensions, not true free-agent signings. Trackers were truncated, so lists are partial: top-40 by AAV per summer is not fully reached for true FAs, and mid/minimum deals are limited. AOL 2025 tracker mixed in older-year deals; only cross-checked items kept. Several 2026 items (LeBron to PHI, Harden CLE) come from a single source and are flagged. Spotrac and Hoops Rumors trackers were not accessible (404/not returned); Basketball-Reference lists no dollar amounts.",
